<article class="article-body">

<p>Cross-functional projects rarely fail inside a single team. They fail at the seams, the handoff between engineering and marketing, the dependency one team didn't know another was waiting on, the decision that got made in a meeting half the stakeholders weren't in. A 100% project success rate across more than ten companies hasn't come from better individual contributors. It's come from making the seams someone's explicit job.</p>

<h2>A RACI isn't bureaucracy, it's where the seams get named</h2>
<p>Most teams resist a formal RACI because it sounds like process for its own sake. In practice, the value isn't the document, it's the conversation that happens while building it: discovering that two people both think they own a decision, or that nobody actually owns one that matters. Thirty minutes spent clarifying who's Responsible, Accountable, Consulted, and Informed on each major piece saves weeks of the ambiguity that quietly kills momentum later.</p>

<h2>A status cadence that reports blockers, not just progress</h2>
<p>Status updates that only report what's done create a false sense of security, because the thing that's actually going to blow the timeline is usually the thing nobody's mentioned yet. A better cadence makes blockers the headline, not a footnote: what's stuck, who's waiting on what, and how long it's been that way. Executive summaries built around blockers get escalated faster than ones built around completed tasks.</p>

<h2>Dependency tracking that's visible to everyone, not just the project lead</h2>
<p>When dependencies live only in one person's head or inbox, the project lead becomes a single point of failure, and anyone downstream of a blocked task has no way to see it coming. A shared dependency map, even a simple one, means a marketing team can see that their launch date depends on an engineering milestone that just slipped, instead of finding out the week of launch.</p>

<h2>A decision log so a disagreement doesn't resurface three weeks later</h2>
<p>Cross-functional projects are full of decisions that get made in one meeting and then quietly re-litigated in a different one because the people in the second meeting weren't in the first. A decision log that's actually referenced, not just written and forgotten, stops this: the decision, the reasoning, and who made it are one search away, which ends most repeat arguments before they start.</p>

<h2>What holds a multi-team project together</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>A RACI built collaboratively</strong>, not handed down, so the ambiguous ownership gets surfaced before it causes a delay.</li>
  <li><strong>A status cadence that leads with blockers</strong>, so problems get escalated while there's still time to fix them.</li>
  <li><strong>A shared dependency view</strong>, so no team is the last to know their work is blocked on someone else's.</li>
  <li><strong>A decision log everyone can search</strong>, so settled questions stay settled.</li>
</ul>

